    body: `<p class="lead">The last mile is where a tidy trip turns into a taxi negotiation at midnight. Here is how we get our guests from each airport to our hotels, with the options in order of what we usually recommend. Prices were checked in September 2026.</p>
<h2 id="rome">Rome</h2>
<h3>Fiumicino (FCO)</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Leonardo Express train</strong> to Roma Termini: 32 minutes non-stop, every 15 minutes, €14 per adult, one child aged 4 to 12 free per adult. Then a 5-minute taxi or 20-minute walk to the Trevi area.</li><li><strong>Fixed-fare taxi</strong> from the official white taxis to anywhere inside the Aurelian walls: €55 for up to four people with luggage (at the time of writing). Best value for three or four travellers or with lots of bags.</li><li><strong>Private transfer</strong> arranged by us: $38 per person, driver with a name board in arrivals.</li></ul>
<h3>Ciampino (CPA)</h3>
<p>Used by some European budget airlines. Fixed-fare taxi to the centre €40; or the bus to Termini for about €7 in 40 minutes.</p>
<h2 id="florence">Florence</h2>
<h3>Peretola / Amerigo Vespucci (FLR)</h3>
<p><strong>Tram T2</strong> from the terminal to the Unità and Alamanni stops beside Santa Maria Novella station: about 20 minutes, every 5 to 10 minutes, standard 90-minute ticket €2 (raised from €1.70 in August 2026). Our hotel is a seven-minute walk from the tram. A taxi is a fixed €25 to €28.</p>
<h3>Pisa (PSA)</h3>
<p>Pisamover shuttle to Pisa Centrale (5 minutes, €5.90) then a regional train to Firenze SMN (about an hour, €9.50). Most US travellers to Florence arrive by train from Rome instead.</p>
<h2 id="venice">Venice</h2>
<h3>Marco Polo (VCE)</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Alilaguna boat</strong>, Blue or Orange line, to San Marco: about 75 minutes, roughly hourly, €18 one way or €32 return, children under 6 free. The San Marco stop is two minutes from our hotel. Blue line also serves the Lido and Fondamente Nove.</li><li><strong>Shared water taxi</strong>: about 30 minutes to the San Marco area, around €35 per person, bookable in advance.</li><li><strong>Private water taxi</strong> to the hotel's own landing: about 30 minutes, €130 to €150 per boat for up to four.</li><li><strong>Bus to Piazzale Roma</strong> (ATVO or ACTV line 5, 20 to 30 minutes, about €10) then vaporetto line 1 down the Grand Canal. Cheapest, slowest, but a good way in if you want the canal ride.</li></ul>
<h3>Treviso (TSF)</h3>
<p>Budget-airline airport 40 km away. ATVO bus to Piazzale Roma, about 70 minutes, €12.</p>
<h2 id="milan">Milan</h2>
<h3>Malpensa (MXP)</h3>
<p><strong>Malpensa Express</strong> train from Terminal 1 (and 2) to Milano Centrale via Milano Porta Garibaldi: 45 to 55 minutes, every 15 to 30 minutes. The adult fare is around €13 (the operator was reviewing fares in 2026; check malpensaexpress.it). Our hotel is next to Porta Garibaldi station. A taxi is a fixed €110 to the city and takes about an hour; there are also coaches to Centrale for €10.</p>
<h3>Linate (LIN)</h3>
<p>Metro line M4 from inside the terminal to San Babila in 12 minutes (€2.20), then M1 to Cadorna and M2 to Garibaldi, or a taxi for €25 to €30.</p>
<h3>Bergamo Orio al Serio (BGY)</h3>
<p>Coaches to Milano Centrale, about an hour, €10 to €12; then M2 two stops to Garibaldi.</p>
<h2 id="tips">General tips</h2>
<ul><li>Official taxis are white with a "TAXI" roof sign and a licence number on the door. Ignore anyone offering a taxi inside the terminal.</li><li>Train tickets bought online or from a machine do not need validating; paper tickets from a counter do, in the green machines on the platform.</li><li>For late arrivals, our Florence hotel has a 24-hour desk and all four hotels can be told your arrival time; we do that for you.</li><li>The EU's Entry/Exit System has been running since April 2026: your first Schengen entry involves fingerprints and a photo at the border, so allow an extra 30 to 60 minutes on arrival at Fiumicino or Malpensa.</li></ul>`
